\details{
The formula is parsed by the same Rust parser the Python port uses. Besides
bare column names, it accepts \code{log(x)}, \code{sqrt(x)}, \code{exp(x)}, and \code{I(x^k)}
(\code{k >= 2}) of a single column; \code{cbind(successes, failures)} on the
left-hand side with \code{family = binomial}; an \code{offset()} term; and term
removal (\code{- 1} / \verb{0 +}). Not accepted: \code{poly()}, arithmetic inside a call,
nested calls, and \code{.} - compute those columns first and pass them by name.
\verb{weights=} together with a \code{cbind()} formula, and \verb{offset=} together with
an \code{offset()} formula term, both error naming "use one". Fixed and random
effects support \code{+}, \code{:}, \code{*}, \code{A/B} nesting, and \code{(1 + x | g)} random-effect
terms with a \strong{full} correlation structure - \code{(x || g)} and intercept-free
RE terms are not fittable by the kernel and raise an error. Contrasts are
always treatment coding with the \strong{first factor level} as base; to change
the base, \code{relevel()} the factor (a \code{contrasts} argument is deliberately
absent). Anything that would silently mean something else - an ordered
factor, a factor with its own \code{contrasts} attribute, or the unordered slot
of \code{options(contrasts = )} set away from \code{"contr.treatment"} - is a clear
error when the column is used in the fixed-effects part of the formula
(random-effect grouping and slope columns are not checked); character and
logical columns are checked the same way as an unordered factor, since
\code{model.matrix()} codes them the same way. Character and logical columns
are converted to factors with lexicographic level order (as \code{factor()}
does, so a logical \code{x} gives levels \code{FALSE}/\code{TRUE} and a coefficient
\code{xTRUE}, as in lme4); a factor's declared level order is honored. A
logical response or \code{offset()} variable stays a 0/1 numeric.

\strong{\code{Gamma()} link trap:} R's \code{Gamma()} family object defaults to
\code{link = "inverse"}, and a family \emph{object} is honored as given - R semantics
win. The string form \code{family = "gamma"} uses the glmm default
\code{link = "log"} instead. The two forms therefore fit different models;
choose deliberately.

\strong{\code{nAGQ} fallback (louder than lme4):} \code{nAGQ > 1} is honored on binomial,
Poisson, negative-binomial and Gamma mixed models with a single grouping
factor and at most 3 random
effects per group. Any other shape \strong{warns and falls back to Laplace}
(\code{nAGQ = 1}) instead of erroring the way \code{lme4::glmer} does - the fit you
get is a Laplace fit, and the warning is the only notice. This mirrors the
Python port so the two ports agree.

\strong{Diagnostic warnings carry a condition class.} Each solver note is raised
as a warning of class \code{"fastglmm_diagnostic"} with a per-kind subclass
(\code{"fastglmm_ill_conditioned"}, \code{"fastglmm_pirls_exhausted"},
\code{"fastglmm_unused_grouping_levels"},
\code{"fastglmm_single_level_grouping_dropped"},
\code{"fastglmm_few_rows_for_random_effects"}, \code{"fastglmm_hessian_se_fallback"},
\code{"fastglmm_se_unavailable"}, \code{"fastglmm_exact_profile_fallback"},
\code{"fastglmm_gradient_search_fallback"},
\code{"fastglmm_precision_limited"}, \code{"fastglmm_non_integer_response"}, \code{"fastglmm_rows_dropped_na"},
\code{"fastglmm_satterthwaite_curvature"}, \code{"fastglmm_variance_at_limit"}; a note from
a newer kernel than this package arrives as \code{"fastglmm_unknown_note"}),
so \code{withCallingHandlers()} and \code{suppressWarnings(classes = )} can select
them without matching message text.

Anything the engine cannot do is an error naming the reason - never a
silently different model. That includes \verb{control=}/\verb{verbose=} and
quasi-likelihood \verb{dispersion=} on binomial/poisson. \code{REML = TRUE} on a model
that is not Gaussian does not apply (the model is fitted by maximum
likelihood): it is ignored with a warning, as an inapplicable \code{nAGQ} is.
}
\examples{
set.seed(1)
n_g <- 30L
g <- factor(rep(seq_len(n_g), each = 10))
t <- rep(seq(0, 0.9, by = 0.1), n_g)
d <- rbinom(n_g * 10L, 1L, 0.4)
u <- rnorm(n_g, sd = 0.8)[as.integer(g)]
y <- rbinom(n_g * 10L, 1L, plogis(-0.5 + 0.7 * t + 0.4 * d + u))
fit <- fastglmm(y ~ t + d + t:d + (1 | g), data.frame(y, t, d, g),
                family = binomial())
fixef(fit)
VarCorr(fit)
}
